WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.979 --> 00:00:04.430
First thing I should say is that the math
community is a wonderful community.

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:10.049
We take joy in what we do, and we love helping
people see the world through a mathematical

00:00:10.049 --> 00:00:11.049
lens.

00:00:11.049 --> 00:00:16.080
At the same time I would say that we suffer
from the same problems that maybe our broader

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.250
culture does.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:23.970
We often idolize achievements, and we think
that — the problem with that is that you

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:27.470
end up thinking that only certain people,
if they're going to achieve at the highest

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:28.810
levels, belong in mathematics.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:35.550
It would be like in saying in music saying
that music is only for the Beethovens and

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:44.580
the Bachs, rather than saying that everybody
can enjoy music and everyone can create music.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:48.930
What happens then in mathematics, if you fall
into that trap, is that you end up thinking

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:55.170
that certain people are math people and certain
people aren't math people.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:56.820
Bias can creep in.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:01.430
We can inadvertently start shepherding some
people to math and other people not to math.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:02.920
And I would say: No.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.890
Math is for everybody.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:10.680
Math helps everybody flourish, no matter what
profession you end up in.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.800
Everybody should learn and enjoy more math.

